            <ArticleTitle>Transapical extirpation of a left ventricular thrombus in Takotsubo cardiomyopathy.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>135</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1186/1749-8090-8-135</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>A 58-year-old Japanese female was referred to our hospital. Although the electrocardiogram showed ST elevation, coronary angiography showed intact coronary artery. We diagnosed Takotsubo cardiomyopathy and a left ventricular thrombus. Anticoagulation was administered; however, the left ventricular thrombus had become mobile and protrusive. We extirpated the left ventricular thrombus via trans-apical approach. Left ventricular thrombus is rare in Takotsubo cardiomyopathy, but these patients are at a higher risk of thromboembolism, especially if the thrombi are mobile and protruding.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
